# G023 — Post it Notes Program

*v2. See `post-it-notes.md` for prose: first data store with CRUD,
identity makes content addressable, search as simplest query, the
note board is the vault's document model stripped to its essentials,
shared memory for agent teams.*

---

## Choreographic Case: Shared Knowledge Base

[shared-knowledge-base
    IN ORDER TO
        let multiple agents create and search notes in a single board
        with each agent's access pattern distinct but non-conflicting
        so the board becomes the team's shared mutable memory

    @board <- [notes[new]]

    concurrent [
        sarah:
            for @task in [tasks[open-today]]
                [notes[create
                        {board: @board,
                         title: @task:title,
                         content: @task:description,
                         tags: ["task", "today"]}]]
    |
        lena:
            @relevant <- [notes[search
                                  {board: @board, query: "daily-note"}]]
            for @n in @relevant
                [lena[summarise-into-briefing {note: @n}]]
    |
        eliana:
            @stale <- [notes[search
                               {board: @board,
                                query: "",
                                filter: {older-than-days: 30}}]]
            for @n in @stale
                [notes[archive {board: @board, id: @n:id}]]
    ]
    join
]

---

## Verb Surface

[notes
    new                              ;; -> empty board
    create   {board, title, content, tags}    ;; -> note-id
    read     {board, id}                       ;; || not-found
    update   {board, id, title, content, tags}
    delete   {board, id}
    archive  {board, id}
    search   {board, query, filter?}            ;; -> [Note]
]

---

## Distinctive Innate idiom: identity `where` same content is a different entity

[notes[create {board: @b, title: @t, content: @c, tags: @tags}]
    IN ORDER TO
        assign a fresh id so content becomes addressable and mutable
        because without identity you cannot say "update that note" —
        only "update every note that looks like this"

    @id <- next-available-id
    store {id: @id, title: @t, content: @c, tags: @tags, created-ms: now}
    return @id
]

Two notes with identical content are still different notes (different
ids). Identity is the difference between a value and an entity. G023
introduces the distinction that the entire CRUD-and-query category
then builds on.

## Distinctive Innate idiom: search as `where` predicate over the collection

[notes[search {board: @b, query: @q, filter: @f}]
    @b:notes
        where title contains @q
           or content contains @q
           or any tag matches @q
        filtered by @f (optional age / author / tag constraints)
        ordered by created-ms descending
    IN ORDER TO
        let every caller bring their own `where` to the collection
        because "find" is the universal read-path every consumer needs
]

---

## Demo

[demo
    @b <- [notes[new]]
    @id1 <- [notes[create
                     {board: @b,
                      title: "Rosetta Stone",
                      content: "second pass at 117/130",
                      tags: ["project", "active"]}]]
    @id2 <- [notes[create
                     {board: @b,
                      title: "Kathryn monthly",
                      content: "cover obligations",
                      tags: ["finance", "recurring"]}]]

    -> [notes[search {board: @b, query: "Rosetta"}]]
    ;; -> [note @id1]

    [notes[update {board: @b, id: @id1,
                    title: "Rosetta Stone",
                    content: "second pass closing",
                    tags: ["project", "active", "closing"]}]]
    -> [notes[read {board: @b, id: @id1}]]:content
    ;; -> "second pass closing"
]
